WEBVTT

NOTE Narrated by a synthetic voice (an ElevenLabs stock voice); the opening disclosure is the macOS Trinoids voice. Text by Phil Renato.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.318
Organic and Disembodied,

00:00:04.318 --> 00:00:08.447
(collecting things and not loving them—drawing things and not making

00:00:08.447 --> 00:00:10.912
things—making things and not using them)

00:00:11.912 --> 00:00:17.362
Creativity requires research. No matter the material, process, scale, intent,

00:00:17.362 --> 00:00:22.096
effect, or other category of activity—nothing is made of vacuum and

00:00:22.096 --> 00:00:27.690
ignorance. Research objects are built of data. And those data have sources that

00:00:27.690 --> 00:00:33.499
vary according to the question or premise to test. Some of my queries or ideas are

00:00:33.499 --> 00:00:38.806
small and specific, some grand and general. If my default is set, it is set

00:00:38.806 --> 00:00:44.328
to ask, “what letters, words, and grammar do I want to use, and to say what?”.

00:00:44.328 --> 00:00:49.349
“The act of reading itself is anything but natural. Human brains weren’t

00:00:49.349 --> 00:00:54.724
designed to read: There is no “reading center” of the brain, and there are no

00:00:54.724 --> 00:00:59.675
“reading genes.” Instead, in order to read, each brain must fashion new

00:00:59.675 --> 00:01:04.343
circuits between parts originally designed to do other things, like

00:01:04.343 --> 00:01:09.859
retrieving the names for objects. These new circuits must not only combine many

00:01:09.859 --> 00:01:15.305
processes from different areas of the brain to form a specialized circuit just

00:01:15.305 --> 00:01:20.821
for reading — in order to become a fluent reader, the circuit also needs to run

00:01:20.821 --> 00:01:26.479
lightning-fast, nearly automatic.” While words are as important as words, and for

00:01:26.479 --> 00:01:31.712
what they lend the sentence, I am primarily interested in the image and the

00:01:31.712 --> 00:01:36.805
object — so I’m using language about objects as proxy for the non-textual

00:01:36.805 --> 00:01:42.109
object — replacing letter and words for shape, line, and value; analogically

00:01:42.109 --> 00:01:46.140
linking grammar for visual composition, emphasis, balance.

00:01:46.140 --> 00:01:50.820
I took the presentations I gave in graduate school way too seriously. The

00:01:50.820 --> 00:01:56.149
first few times you do anything, you tend to both be terrible at it and give it too

00:01:56.149 --> 00:02:00.894
much importance. In the more than ten years since, I’ve mythologized these

00:02:00.894 --> 00:02:06.094
presentations, invented constraints that didn’t necessarily exist, and replaced a

00:02:06.094 --> 00:02:11.228
student exercise with a semi-pro dogma; swapping a spark for an inferno. And one

00:02:11.228 --> 00:02:16.233
that sometimes threatens to consume the very thing it was meant to illuminate.

00:02:16.233 --> 00:02:21.507
The sources for the sources I am in the habit of collecting vary according to the

00:02:21.507 --> 00:02:25.660
period and the question. When I was starting out, they included:

00:02:25.660 --> 00:02:27.657
The stuff other metalsmiths made,

00:02:27.657 --> 00:02:29.979
The stuff people in other countries wore,

00:02:29.979 --> 00:02:31.604
The stuff sculptors made,

00:02:31.604 --> 00:02:35.784
The stuff great designers drew and manufacturers made,

00:02:35.784 --> 00:02:37.967
The stuff around me in the studio,

00:02:37.967 --> 00:02:39.778
The stuff painters made,

00:02:39.778 --> 00:02:45.037
The stuff I found in UofM and UW’s Property Disposition; mainly medical

00:02:45.037 --> 00:02:46.465
tools and supplies,

00:02:46.465 --> 00:02:49.205
The stuff that people wrote about some of the above,

00:02:49.205 --> 00:02:53.986
The stuff that people wrote about culture, specifically the history of the

00:02:53.986 --> 00:02:54.313
West.

00:02:54.313 --> 00:02:59.397
And fifteen years later they have shifted and have been refined, primarily to

00:02:59.397 --> 00:02:59.932
include:

00:02:59.932 --> 00:03:01.232
Popular stuff,

00:03:01.232 --> 00:03:02.532
Toys that teach,

00:03:02.532 --> 00:03:03.925
Fetishelectronica,

00:03:03.925 --> 00:03:04.807
Organics,

00:03:04.807 --> 00:03:06.479
Signs and sign making,

00:03:06.479 --> 00:03:08.615
Borders and interfaces.

00:03:08.615 --> 00:03:13.740
In the visual arts, we see a number of students who are drawing (and writing)

00:03:13.740 --> 00:03:19.135
fixated; covering every centimeter of any paper with iterative silhouettes, nouns

00:03:19.135 --> 00:03:23.721
on repeat, and glyphs of unknown destination. These hypergraphics are

00:03:23.721 --> 00:03:28.981
covering the white with noise, or are anxiously processing what they’re hearing

00:03:28.981 --> 00:03:34.511
with a task to keep time from passing, or are just working through large constructs

00:03:34.511 --> 00:03:39.299
writ small. My notebook pages and Post-Its® are dumbbell curls and bench

00:03:39.299 --> 00:03:44.290
presses; things involved in the process of building muscle, but never to be

00:03:44.290 --> 00:03:49.415
confused for the bicep or pectorals. There is a similous routine for those of

00:03:49.415 --> 00:03:54.405
us looking at the world through a viewfinder. Photomanics capture the world

00:03:54.405 --> 00:03:59.665
to avoid having to deal with it. But we also have a sortable record that is, at

00:03:59.665 --> 00:04:04.521
once, a great quantity to search and sort, and easier to sort into useful

00:04:04.521 --> 00:04:04.993
albums.

00:04:04.993 --> 00:04:10.097
What’s the difference between curiosity and obsession, between rigorous research

00:04:10.097 --> 00:04:15.137
and hackneyed habit, between unveiling the ignored beauty of the designed world

00:04:15.137 --> 00:04:19.983
and indulging a pointless idiosyncrasy? I’ve been composing a new world in a

00:04:19.983 --> 00:04:24.376
camera’s rectangle for twenty years now, and these data have survived

00:04:24.376 --> 00:04:29.416
transfiguration from grain to pixels. They’ve accelerated from a slow, careful,

00:04:29.416 --> 00:04:34.520
stewarding of precious cans of film to the near oppressive freedom of being able

00:04:34.520 --> 00:04:38.784
to take and store thousands of consequenceless images at a time; on

00:04:38.784 --> 00:04:43.824
cheaper and better devices that can zoom across buildings or focus on bees that

00:04:43.824 --> 00:04:44.793
rest on a lens.

00:04:44.793 --> 00:04:50.391
I’ve managed to backup one hundred four thousand, three hundred eight photographs

00:04:50.391 --> 00:04:56.059
as of 9:10pm tonight. They are on Flickr, in my Dropbox, and on two external LaCie

00:04:56.059 --> 00:05:01.657
1TB Firewire hard drives. And then there are the gigabytes of RAW and edited TIFF

00:05:01.657 --> 00:05:06.976
files, layouts of postcard designs and other assets I’ve used images to make.

00:05:06.976 --> 00:05:12.364
They are loosely cared for, but mainly as a mass; most of them have their EXIF

00:05:12.364 --> 00:05:17.682
data, but many lack titles and tags and discrete albums that could be said to

00:05:17.682 --> 00:05:22.930
give this photomass any immediately useful form — to transform the data into

00:05:22.930 --> 00:05:28.598
information. This is the evidence of more than a hundred thousand minute decisions

00:05:28.598 --> 00:05:33.777
while traveling, shopping, walking, working out, studying, tending animals,

00:05:33.777 --> 00:05:39.445
working on cars, and randomly focused on the second of four screws with a hex head

00:05:39.445 --> 00:05:41.264
rather than a slotted cap.

00:05:41.264 --> 00:05:46.029
Recent studies that have shown that people who take photographs of their

00:05:46.029 --> 00:05:51.129
activities remember them less well than people who concentrate on the moment.

00:05:51.129 --> 00:05:56.296
This makes sense, and I’m sure sustains experimental scrutiny as long as a few

00:05:56.296 --> 00:06:00.927
conditions are met: the images are not later reviewed (or needed,) the

00:06:00.927 --> 00:06:05.625
photographer is not also concentrating (but near randomly composing and

00:06:05.625 --> 00:06:10.524
shooting,) and the non-photographer is skilled at paying attention without

00:06:10.524 --> 00:06:15.624
documenting. No one ever thinks a study applies to them, but this one doesn’t

00:06:15.624 --> 00:06:20.724
apply to me. While photography has all kinds of differences from sketching or

00:06:20.724 --> 00:06:26.160
journaling — it has the same potential — and it’s hard to argue that putting items

00:06:26.160 --> 00:06:31.193
in a list (compressing things) makes the listmaker less aware of the listed.

00:06:31.193 --> 00:06:36.562
Putting items into an order and reviewing them in a new context enriches memory —

00:06:36.562 --> 00:06:41.796
it is memory. As long as the order is reviewed. I review (and am in the process

00:06:41.796 --> 00:06:47.098
of going back through my images for this writing), and I pay close attention (at

00:06:47.098 --> 00:06:52.601
least to the parts I want to learn from.) Almost each of the images in my knowledge

00:06:52.601 --> 00:06:57.768
base is something I captured — and each represents something I witnessed, took

00:06:57.768 --> 00:07:03.137
note of, and remember (although not with photographic precision, paradoxically.).

00:07:03.137 --> 00:07:08.251
I’ve never finished reading Proust’s Remembrance of Things Past, but I have

00:07:08.251 --> 00:07:13.226
read the first book, Swann’s Way. If there is one narrative thread I held

00:07:13.226 --> 00:07:18.616
through those pages it is that memory is complicated, interconnected, fallible.

00:07:18.616 --> 00:07:23.799
It’s hard for me to believe in fixed memory, rather I accumulate things that

00:07:23.799 --> 00:07:28.774
will become memory elements, mements; things that ‘succumb by yielding to

00:07:28.774 --> 00:07:33.611
nervous impulses’ to bond to other elements they come upon. I await the

00:07:33.611 --> 00:07:39.001
impulse to start recalling in ecstatic images ‘the vicissitudes of life.’ But I

00:07:39.001 --> 00:07:44.253
don’t have Proust’s patience, or faith that toast or a cookie, soaked in tea,

00:07:44.253 --> 00:07:49.159
will suppress my feelings of mediocrity or mortality, as it did for him.

00:07:49.159 --> 00:07:54.623
My study collection and photographs are columns in a data set that parallels the

00:07:54.623 --> 00:07:59.880
sum of my experience, which in turn forms the neural wiring where my evolving

00:07:59.880 --> 00:08:04.652
theory of design is encoded. Other columns contain elements with fixed

00:08:04.652 --> 00:08:09.632
values, those maximal references that I entered at some early point in my

00:08:09.632 --> 00:08:14.820
education, and then columns (like images and objects) that have quantitative

00:08:14.820 --> 00:08:20.146
values — which are then qualified with an evaluative flag when reviewed. These

00:08:20.146 --> 00:08:25.333
types of columns are continuously added to the set, but only reviewed when a

00:08:25.333 --> 00:08:30.728
subsequent entry sufficiently overlaps the rows. Duplication compounds interest

00:08:30.728 --> 00:08:36.192
in the subject or detail; and raises the possibility not only that the reference

00:08:36.192 --> 00:08:38.060
will be sticky, but that a,

00:08:38.060 --> 00:08:39.593
“confused by”.

00:08:39.593 --> 00:08:41.265
‘frustrated by’.

00:08:41.265 --> 00:08:42.519
“ignorant of”.

00:08:42.519 --> 00:08:44.098
‘check back in’.

00:08:44.098 --> 00:08:47.674
‘there is something there, but’.

00:08:47.674 --> 00:08:49.439
‘how was this made?’.

00:08:49.439 --> 00:08:51.761
“where have I seen this before”.

00:08:51.761 --> 00:08:53.619
“I love this”.

00:08:53.619 --> 00:08:56.870
‘I have never seen anything like this!’.

00:08:56.870 --> 00:08:58.495
‘how does it work?’.

00:08:58.495 --> 00:09:00.585
“how much did it cost?”.

00:09:00.585 --> 00:09:02.628
“who was this designed for?”.

00:09:02.628 --> 00:09:04.532
“what were they thinking”.

00:09:04.532 --> 00:09:06.668
“that has to be an accident”.

00:09:06.668 --> 00:09:08.201
‘that looks fragile’.

00:09:08.201 --> 00:09:10.244
“I could use that,” or just,

00:09:10.244 --> 00:09:12.380
“that’s beautiful”.

00:09:12.380 --> 00:09:16.420
flag will be placed when floating sums are reached.

00:09:16.420 --> 00:09:21.568
While my first presentation using materials of this type was while earning

00:09:21.568 --> 00:09:26.928
my MFA, the basic exercise was first practiced in my (short-lived) vocational

00:09:26.928 --> 00:09:32.146
Advertising Design training in high school. For three hours a day, we would

00:09:32.146 --> 00:09:37.717
conceive, draw, and pitch ideas to sell products without the benefit of a single

00:09:37.717 --> 00:09:42.442
Macintosh. These campaigns were fabricated from pieces broken off of

00:09:42.442 --> 00:09:47.943
television and from magazines that were similar to those we were mock-designing

00:09:47.943 --> 00:09:53.091
for. We talked and wrote, sketched, pulled rough references, composed mood

00:09:53.091 --> 00:09:57.886
boards, critiqued, developed rough composites, and then painstakingly

00:09:57.886 --> 00:10:03.457
rendered our final designs with markers on board. At first, National Geographic,

00:10:03.457 --> 00:10:08.887
Time, and Car and Driver supplied almost everything—we were remixing before we

00:10:08.887 --> 00:10:14.529
knew the term. By the middle of our first year, however, we were asked to use our

00:10:14.529 --> 00:10:19.254
primitive cameras to do primary research/to collect original assets.

00:10:19.254 --> 00:10:23.616
Because of the cost of film, our sophomoric technical and aesthetic

00:10:23.616 --> 00:10:28.506
skills, and our lack of discipline in finding good locations and subjects —

00:10:28.506 --> 00:10:33.925
most of us continued using stolen imagery to layer into our ‘original’ work. But we

00:10:33.925 --> 00:10:39.014
still wished we had crafted more of the story ourselves. By the time I entered

00:10:39.014 --> 00:10:44.301
college the seed of becoming proficient at capturing things I could later exploit

00:10:44.301 --> 00:10:45.028
was a weed.

00:10:45.028 --> 00:10:50.123
This curve has circled in the years since—the technical and storage and

00:10:50.123 --> 00:10:56.091
transportation and organizational aspects are no longer crippling, even as the body

00:10:56.091 --> 00:11:01.840
of images has become grotesquely obese. So I horde them because I can. I collect

00:11:01.840 --> 00:11:07.226
and covet images of things that I’m thinking about, dump them into publicly

00:11:07.226 --> 00:11:12.248
available or private sets on Flickr—holding onto them for futures that

00:11:12.248 --> 00:11:17.633
may one day come. McLuhan observed that “far from regarding our pictures as

00:11:17.633 --> 00:11:23.383
incentives to mayhem and revolution, we take them as solace and compensation, or

00:11:23.383 --> 00:11:26.731
as a form of deferred payment by daydreaming.”.

00:11:26.731 --> 00:11:32.452
I use pictures to argue, to share with students who need images of experiences

00:11:32.452 --> 00:11:37.505
we’ve had together for their presentations, as examples of materials,

00:11:37.505 --> 00:11:43.078
processes, forms, or ideas for lectures. I use discrete images as particular

00:11:43.078 --> 00:11:48.650
references for works in process, I use groups of them to choose palettes and

00:11:48.650 --> 00:11:54.149
consider details, and I use them to develop source videos every year or so.

00:11:54.149 --> 00:11:55.709
Images are the world.

00:11:55.709 --> 00:12:02.022
“Picture a 13-year-old boy sitting in the living room of his family home doing his

00:12:02.022 --> 00:12:07.664
math assignment while wearing his Walkman headphones or watching MTV. He enjoys the

00:12:07.664 --> 00:12:12.755
liberties hard won over centuries by the alliance of philosophic genius and

00:12:12.755 --> 00:12:17.846
political heroism, consecrated by the blood of martyrs; he is provided with

00:12:17.846 --> 00:12:22.800
comfort and leisure by the most productive economy ever known to mankind;

00:12:22.800 --> 00:12:28.029
science has penetrated the secrets of nature in order to provide him with the

00:12:28.029 --> 00:12:33.601
marvelous, lifelike electronic sound and image reproduction he is enjoying. And in

00:12:33.601 --> 00:12:38.968
what does progress culminate? A pubescent child whose body throbs with orgasmic

00:12:38.968 --> 00:12:43.509
rhythms; whose feelings are made articulate in hymns to the joys of

00:12:43.509 --> 00:12:48.806
onanism or the killing of parents; whose ambition is to win fame and wealth in

00:12:48.806 --> 00:12:53.898
imitating the drag queen who makes the music. In short, life is made into a

00:12:53.898 --> 00:12:57.888
nonstop, commercially prepackaged masturbational fantasy.”.

00:12:57.888 --> 00:13:07.040
Footnote 8. Allan Bloom, June 7, 1987, Is Rock Music Rotting our Kids’ Minds?, The

00:13:07.040 --> 00:13:08.848
Washington Post.

00:13:08.848 --> 00:13:13.603
This purple diatribe was published in the Washington Post in 1987, and my

00:13:13.603 --> 00:13:18.160
grandmother read it, along with the eventual book that this seed would

00:13:18.160 --> 00:13:22.782
germinate into, The Closing of the American Mind. She wasn’t much of an

00:13:22.782 --> 00:13:27.603
intellectual, but she did consider herself a classicist—at least when this

00:13:27.603 --> 00:13:32.820
conformed to Catholic dogma. Because I was born in 1974, I was the thirteen year

00:13:32.820 --> 00:13:37.773
old being talked about. When she made me read it at her kitchen table, I was

00:13:37.773 --> 00:13:42.925
transitioning from Michael Jackson to punk rock—and would much rather listen to

00:13:42.925 --> 00:13:47.944
David Bowie than watch a piano recital on PBS. I didn’t understand all of the

00:13:47.944 --> 00:13:53.161
words, but with the aid of a dictionary was able to get through and make out the

00:13:53.161 --> 00:13:57.585
theme. Allan Bloom can suck an old pimpled dick, but this was pretty

00:13:57.585 --> 00:14:02.472
powerful writing. I loved its form, though I never agreed with it—because I

00:14:02.472 --> 00:14:05.180
was inside the body he was writing about.

00:14:05.180 --> 00:14:10.865
I was seduced by music and the characters that created and were created by it. I

00:14:10.865 --> 00:14:16.335
watched Sinead O’Connor cover Prince’s Nothing Compares 2 U on near repeat on

00:14:16.335 --> 00:14:22.092
MTV a few years after Bloom’s essay was written. And not only did I enjoy it, not

00:14:22.092 --> 00:14:27.993
only did I ‘throb with orgasmic rhythms,’ but I was transformed by it. Those lyrics

00:14:27.993 --> 00:14:33.679
helped me get through my first teenage romantic relationships without implosion,

00:14:33.679 --> 00:14:39.580
and learn (slowly) to hear, make sense of and use poetry. Without them I would have

00:14:39.580 --> 00:14:42.099
been ‘like a bird without a song.’.

00:14:42.099 --> 00:14:46.662
I survived drowning in my onanistic music habits, and about the time Jane’s

00:14:46.662 --> 00:14:51.102
Addiction was replacing Perry’s papier-mâché sculpture with a copy of the

00:14:51.102 --> 00:14:55.666
first amendment on the cover of their second major album, I was learning to

00:14:55.666 --> 00:14:59.736
make aesthetic judgments. Writing terrible poems and short stories,

00:14:59.736 --> 00:15:04.669
painting punky self-portraits and taking artsy photographs with me in the center.

00:15:04.669 --> 00:15:09.434
A few years later, finally at a university (and an art major,) I saw an

00:15:09.434 --> 00:15:14.607
already obsolete Video Toaster in a decrepit computer lab and was transfixed.

00:15:14.607 --> 00:15:19.303
At first, I thought of recording and editing a video for my then-band,

00:15:19.303 --> 00:15:24.681
Midori—but getting time in the studio was difficult for students that weren’t in

00:15:24.681 --> 00:15:29.718
the School of Communications. And then the band broke up—so that star never

00:15:29.718 --> 00:15:35.095
shone. But I did eventually find the Mac lab upstairs, and scored a beta version

00:15:35.095 --> 00:15:39.723
of what would become Final Cut Pro that one of my professors got from

00:15:39.723 --> 00:15:43.399
Macromedia/Apple that he wouldn’t find the time to use.

00:15:43.399 --> 00:15:48.849
Every year, a few months out from an opportunity to share what I’ve been doing

00:15:48.849 --> 00:15:54.370
with some audience I cut together new ninety secondish videos about my sources;

00:15:54.370 --> 00:15:59.961
some aspect of my process, or the coming together of a particular piece. In each

00:15:59.961 --> 00:16:04.916
there are dozens to hundreds of stills and short videos — usually being

00:16:04.916 --> 00:16:10.224
digitally panned and zoomed, rotated and transitioned into one another. Some

00:16:10.224 --> 00:16:14.754
depict breaking waves on the California shore, some the bulb of a

00:16:14.754 --> 00:16:20.063
sphygmomanometer, some a group of Pepsi bottles on a shelf, some slow motion

00:16:20.063 --> 00:16:25.725
streams of snow on a stream while yellow lines pass, some the front right quarter

00:16:25.725 --> 00:16:31.033
of a Pontiac Trans Am and some the latch mechanism on a Powerbook 2400c. The

00:16:31.033 --> 00:16:36.554
images are kind of about those things, or my view on those things, but they are

00:16:36.554 --> 00:16:41.721
also about water, blood, sugar, speed, light and transition. They are sex,

00:16:41.721 --> 00:16:46.746
death, hyperactivity, sadness and procrastination. And paradise, cancer,

00:16:46.746 --> 00:16:51.842
marketing, affectation, youth and transparency. Each image conjuring, for

00:16:51.842 --> 00:16:57.576
me, the concrete and the abstract. I have immediate and delayed reactions to each,

00:16:57.576 --> 00:17:02.813
and to the associations of the fading agglomerations, that I could not know

00:17:02.813 --> 00:17:04.158
except by reaction.

00:17:04.158 --> 00:17:09.086
These videos are mine, for me. They are how I periodically check in and

00:17:09.086 --> 00:17:14.578
concentrate on some of the things I’ve paid attention to. Each has a soundtrack

00:17:14.578 --> 00:17:20.140
— usually a snippet of a song I have on repeat in my studio. And this soundtrack

00:17:20.140 --> 00:17:25.561
is one of the many things people have reacted to after my artist lecture. I’ve

00:17:25.561 --> 00:17:30.912
had some positive feedback — but I’ve also had a nagging complaint, expressed

00:17:30.912 --> 00:17:36.193
primarily in private that ‘the music is distracting.’ Especially the music I

00:17:36.193 --> 00:17:39.220
choose; contemporary and with a human voice.

00:17:39.220 --> 00:17:44.188
I understand that people have a hard time parsing new information when it is

00:17:44.188 --> 00:17:48.891
heavily layered—that hearing a song you don’t know, or know well, or are

00:17:48.891 --> 00:17:54.057
surprised to know, makes it difficult to follow a fast-paced stream of animated

00:17:54.057 --> 00:17:58.694
images in the center of a 45-minute lecture by a cursing, gesticulating

00:17:58.694 --> 00:18:03.728
Italian American guy. But Boy Sets Fire and Li’l Wayne and The Weeknd are not

00:18:03.728 --> 00:18:08.563
just there to keep the silence from masticating us, they are there to give

00:18:08.563 --> 00:18:13.531
context to the search and the sorting these videos represent—they are one of

00:18:13.531 --> 00:18:18.432
the sources; primarily standing in for the word without the rhythm-breaking

00:18:18.432 --> 00:18:23.400
spectacle of me reading quotations in the middle of my lecture. And they are

00:18:23.400 --> 00:18:28.434
ever-present sources in my life—I hear music even when my headphones are off.

00:18:28.434 --> 00:18:33.137
David Ulin remarked that “to read, we need a certain kind of silence, an

00:18:33.137 --> 00:18:38.038
ability to filter out the noise.” I agree—but would qualify certain silence

00:18:38.038 --> 00:18:43.470
as not necessarily being about sound, but the filter. The important thing is that I

00:18:43.470 --> 00:18:48.835
can find a way to engage with ideas that happen to be in the form of books, in the

00:18:48.835 --> 00:18:54.002
case of Ulin’s example. If I find that something about my world is noisy in the

00:18:54.002 --> 00:18:59.433
distracting sense—I need to find a way to cancel it out or ignore it. And the noise

00:18:59.433 --> 00:19:04.268
that fades when I’m reading or writing is actually the very music I set my

00:19:04.268 --> 00:19:09.700
presentations to. Once I know the work, I prefer the bold cacophony Of Monsters and

00:19:09.700 --> 00:19:14.866
Men to the breathing of the guy sitting behind me at the library, or the spiral

00:19:14.866 --> 00:19:19.701
patterns and tree branches of the wallpaper, or the bird shit on the trunk

00:19:19.701 --> 00:19:25.000
of the Ford Focus out the window. Music is soundtrack—even without being composed

00:19:25.000 --> 00:19:29.703
explicitly for the events I am experiencing, it helps bring whatever I’m

00:19:29.703 --> 00:19:35.068
working on into sharpness and pushes the rest into the back of a relatively narrow

00:19:35.068 --> 00:19:36.062
depth of field.

00:19:36.062 --> 00:19:41.808
The former president of the college where I teach, Oliver Evans, wrote a book about

00:19:41.808 --> 00:19:47.064
the minor writer George Henry Boker in 1984. In a passage setting up Boker’s

00:19:47.064 --> 00:19:52.390
critical analysis of ‘what makes for an appropriate subject,’ Evans wrote “An

00:19:52.390 --> 00:19:57.927
artist needed to escape the present not only because the present is distracting,

00:19:57.927 --> 00:20:03.533
but also because it lacks the materials for art.” With Boker supporting, “Get out

00:20:03.533 --> 00:20:09.069
of your age as much as you can.” But, as he fleshes out characters in one of his

00:20:09.069 --> 00:20:14.465
plays, it’s hard not to see Boker talking about his preoccupation with subject

00:20:14.465 --> 00:20:20.001
making “These mental weeds grow on the surface of inactive peace. I’m haunted by

00:20:20.001 --> 00:20:25.538
myself. Thought preys on thought. My mind seems crowded in the hideous mold that

00:20:25.538 --> 00:20:26.729
shaped my body.”.

00:20:26.729 --> 00:20:32.240
My “hideous mold” is not just my body, but the things I’ve stuffed it with; the

00:20:32.240 --> 00:20:38.034
relationships, books, podcasts, artworks, movies, music, articles, television, blog

00:20:38.034 --> 00:20:43.263
posts, social media, and near panoramic views of daily life. My short video

00:20:43.263 --> 00:20:48.775
projects help me to make sense of these things in situ, and communicate same to

00:20:48.775 --> 00:20:54.286
others. I am conscious that the standards of representation in my field and the

00:20:54.286 --> 00:20:59.797
“blood of the martyrs” that Bloom talked about are a little out of step with my

00:20:59.797 --> 00:21:05.238
practice. And I’m working to revere them a little less, to turn the music up a

00:21:05.238 --> 00:21:09.407
little louder (and make sure there is a subwoofer present.).

00:21:09.407 --> 00:21:14.461
The medium can be defined in any way that’s useful in the breaking down and

00:21:14.461 --> 00:21:19.856
understanding of its nature and effects. The wheel extends the foot and arms, as

00:21:19.856 --> 00:21:25.251
well as the bucket — which extends the hands. The barrow is then extended by the

00:21:25.251 --> 00:21:30.168
multi-wheeled cart. The carriage took their place, then the car, then the

00:21:30.168 --> 00:21:35.495
eighteen wheeler. The swim becomes the boat, the aircraft, the carrier and then

00:21:35.495 --> 00:21:40.753
the rocket. Each is category and contents — multiplying the ability to carry a

00:21:40.753 --> 00:21:46.080
thing from one place to another. They are generalized media — as are the earth,

00:21:46.080 --> 00:21:51.270
water and space they move through. Each is also a tool, something designed or

00:21:51.270 --> 00:21:56.051
used to accomplish particular purposes — to stack up money, for sex and

00:21:56.051 --> 00:22:01.104
companionship and to lock these things behind bullets. Media are platforms,

00:22:01.104 --> 00:22:06.295
enabling industries, building systems, defining space. The thing that travels

00:22:06.295 --> 00:22:08.480
has things traveling through it.

00:22:08.480 --> 00:22:13.307
“I want you staplegunned right to my side, all of the time”.

00:22:13.307 --> 00:22:18.481
Footnote 9. The Spill Canvas, Staplegunned, from the album One Fell

00:22:18.481 --> 00:22:23.106
Swoop… “…the chemistry between us could destroy this place”.

00:22:23.106 --> 00:22:28.693
Glue is a dirty word; a cheap, simple, smelly goo that webs our fingers and

00:22:28.693 --> 00:22:34.205
oozes all over surfaces that don’t want to stick together. We are all time

00:22:34.205 --> 00:22:40.245
binders—on a continuum from almost never to near forever. Deep inside, everything

00:22:40.245 --> 00:22:45.832
is motion, prevented from flying into cold, dark, slowness with the grip of

00:22:45.832 --> 00:22:51.872
electromagchemical bonds, smaller than doubt. Some agents are stronger than the

00:22:51.872 --> 00:22:57.308
things they bond together, some without the bite to fix something waxy to

00:22:57.308 --> 00:23:03.424
something absorbent, others so toxic that they eat the things they join. The mass,

00:23:03.424 --> 00:23:09.313
its elements, at distance, distorting space, tugging on the inert, pleading for

00:23:09.313 --> 00:23:09.917
a crash,

00:23:09.917 --> 00:23:16.124
A short, muscle-bound man stood sweaty in his bikini briefs, unzipping a tan canvas

00:23:16.124 --> 00:23:21.726
case in the back of a small walk-in closet. He returned to face his two—too

00:23:21.726 --> 00:23:27.555
tired sons and an already bruised wife, pointing his Vietnam-era pistol at his

00:23:27.555 --> 00:23:28.539
right temple.

00:23:28.539 --> 00:23:33.238
“Is this what you want? Is this what I have to do to get you to show me some

00:23:33.238 --> 00:23:37.624
goddamned respect?!” he asked, whiskey-stupid, demanding an answer over

00:23:37.624 --> 00:23:40.381
and over in that charming way that drunks do.

00:23:40.381 --> 00:23:45.146
“No, no, please no,” we replied in chorus. We wanted our stucco ceiling

00:23:45.146 --> 00:23:50.456
stained red, but were too afraid to say it. We were standing in the middle of a

00:23:50.456 --> 00:23:55.834
wood paneled master bedroom, surrounded by the middlest of classes. Small Lakes,

00:23:55.834 --> 00:24:00.463
Monte Carlos, Coney Islands. We had barbecues, and uncles fought over

00:24:00.463 --> 00:24:05.841
grilling duties. We went on vacations, and were beaten for ordering too much ice

00:24:05.841 --> 00:24:11.151
cream. We stayed awake in bed while our father worked the night shift—wondering

00:24:11.151 --> 00:24:14.282
what would happen if we put ourselves to sleep.

00:24:14.282 --> 00:24:19.538
A man returns home late from work, a doberman steals a hot dog and has her

00:24:19.538 --> 00:24:25.370
ribs broken for it, her cries wake young boys, a woman defends them and is beaten,

00:24:25.370 --> 00:24:30.626
a bullet is chambered. Time flows forward, never back — except in dream. I

00:24:30.626 --> 00:24:36.387
can see his skull fragments being ground into the carpet, I can taste the iron in

00:24:36.387 --> 00:24:42.147
his blood dripping down our walls and I fantasize about the peace in the violence

00:24:42.147 --> 00:24:47.547
of his death; recursively replaying it while Synchronicity spins on my first

00:24:47.547 --> 00:24:48.555
record player.

00:24:48.555 --> 00:24:53.452
“...Guns are like vacuum cleaners operating in the direction of life — pull

00:24:53.452 --> 00:24:58.746
the trigger and bullets are sucked back out of the recently dead into the barrel,

00:24:58.746 --> 00:25:03.908
and the Great Irreversible is actually reversed...” — Thomas Pynchon, Gravity’s

00:25:03.908 --> 00:25:04.437
Rainbow.

00:25:04.437 --> 00:25:10.424
No surface has depth. Some allow our eyes to penetrate their hard or permeable

00:25:10.424 --> 00:25:16.412
skins in order to see inside — but they are all interfaces between two worlds;

00:25:16.412 --> 00:25:22.321
with conceptual vectors pointing in opposite directions. Where one reflects a

00:25:22.321 --> 00:25:28.582
truth, another absorbs a lie; a skin keeps out the world and in the sap.

00:25:28.582 --> 00:25:33.541
“He falls in love with a thing that has no substance; and he thinks that to be a

00:25:33.541 --> 00:25:35.362
body which is but a shadow.”.

00:25:35.362 --> 00:25:40.935
Footnote 10. Ovid, Metamorphoses, Narcissus.

00:25:40.935 --> 00:25:46.615
Our fights and flights are consanguine — cardinal warp and weft. As we buoy, these

00:25:46.615 --> 00:25:51.805
twins grow listless. As we drown they thrash — but without predator or path

00:25:51.805 --> 00:25:57.064
they enjoy our bodies by consuming them. Our last moments are drowsy mirrors

00:25:57.064 --> 00:26:02.604
smashed by hyperarousal; what might be sharp reflections dull as blood is stolen

00:26:02.604 --> 00:26:05.269
from our minds to let muscles breathe.

00:26:05.269 --> 00:26:11.617
The medium slows Echo’s refrain, and her symmetric ‘farewell’ impregnates a barren

00:26:11.617 --> 00:26:17.260
puddle. Whatever sound is born is suffocated. Good love, found, aspirates

00:26:17.260 --> 00:26:23.374
in the imagination of our search for the perfect. We can see it in still water,

00:26:23.374 --> 00:26:26.352
but as we reach in the wave blinds us.

00:26:26.352 --> 00:26:29.974
Void Free, Class A Surfaces,

00:26:29.974 --> 00:26:33.689
(object as love, cast in polycarbonate).

00:26:33.689 --> 00:26:38.722
Six generations of Corvettes were terminated by twins and quadruplets of

00:26:38.722 --> 00:26:44.252
round, oval and near round rectangular lights. People lost their minds when the

00:26:44.252 --> 00:26:49.781
2014 Stingray showed four inset, canted rhombi with chamfered bottom corners as

00:26:49.781 --> 00:26:55.169
tail lamps. As much veneration as Tom Peters showed to the fashion, branding,

00:26:55.169 --> 00:27:00.840
badging, edges, body lines and fastback glass of the original, this grandchild of

00:27:00.840 --> 00:27:06.015
the sixty-one year old Corvette was pounded by purists for what it wore on

00:27:06.015 --> 00:27:11.545
its ass — as if the signature were now illegible. Some of the outrage seemed to

00:27:11.545 --> 00:27:16.932
center on how much it owed to the nouveau Camaro — a family resemblance being

00:27:16.932 --> 00:27:22.462
grafted from the Australian bastard ZetaF body onto the American halo Corvette.

00:27:22.462 --> 00:27:27.779
Some of it might have been the frown in the corners, as the horizontal vents

00:27:27.779 --> 00:27:33.024
blended into the outer edge of the clear red lenses. This amateur nostalgia

00:27:33.024 --> 00:27:38.341
notwithstanding, GM brought this vehicle from trendy retro rod to accessible

00:27:38.341 --> 00:27:44.154
exotic. Surrounded by black metallic clad bezels, the new Corvette tail lights glow

00:27:44.154 --> 00:27:49.542
forward in Star Trek warp streams while still connecting this mid-life, upper

00:27:49.542 --> 00:27:54.717
middle-class two-seater to the space adventuring child that’s been wasting

00:27:54.717 --> 00:27:56.631
within its would be driver.

00:27:56.631 --> 00:28:02.570
“We think that grass is green, that stones are hard, and that snow is cold.

00:28:02.570 --> 00:28:08.331
But physics assures us that the greenness of grass, the hardness of stones, and the

00:28:08.331 --> 00:28:13.740
coldness of snow are not the greenness, hardness, and coldness that we know in

00:28:13.740 --> 00:28:19.009
our experience, but something very different. The observer, when he seems to

00:28:19.009 --> 00:28:24.068
himself to be observing a stone, is really, if physics is to be believed,

00:28:24.068 --> 00:28:27.510
observing the effects of the stone upon himself.”.

00:28:27.510 --> 00:28:35.681
Footnote 11. Bertrand Russell, An Inquiry Into Meaning and Truth, George Allen and

00:28:35.681 --> 00:28:36.891
Unwin, 1940.

00:28:36.891 --> 00:28:42.352
I’d first read Bertrand Russell’s name when his book Principia Mathematica was

00:28:42.352 --> 00:28:47.813
discussed in Gödel, Escher, Bach, while setting up some of the foundations for

00:28:47.813 --> 00:28:53.558
Hoftstadter’s Strange Loops theories. The math and formal logic in GEB were mostly

00:28:53.558 --> 00:28:58.807
things I had to ignore in order to finish it, but I do recall that Gödel (a

00:28:58.807 --> 00:29:04.197
mathematician used as a model in GEB) situated his incompleteness theorems by

00:29:04.197 --> 00:29:09.587
developing a system to understand one of the observations Russell made — that

00:29:09.587 --> 00:29:14.765
there are inherent blind spots in set theory. Gödel generalized the liar’s

00:29:14.765 --> 00:29:20.297
paradox with an apparent illogical truth, “this statement is not provable.” The

00:29:20.297 --> 00:29:25.687
system can’t prove that the system is consistent. This extension of Russell’s

00:29:25.687 --> 00:29:31.290
“effects of the stone upon himself” idea made my heart beat faster. I started to

00:29:31.290 --> 00:29:37.106
consume him, and felt a quickening as the quote continued “Thus science seems to be

00:29:37.106 --> 00:29:42.638
at war with itself: when it most means to be objective, it finds itself plunged

00:29:42.638 --> 00:29:48.454
into subjectivity against its will. Naive realism leads to physics, and physics, if

00:29:48.454 --> 00:29:53.560
true, shows that naive realism is false naive realism, if true, is false:

00:29:53.560 --> 00:29:58.950
therefore, it is false. And, therefore, the behaviorist, when he thinks he is

00:29:58.950 --> 00:30:04.412
recording observations about the outer world, is really recording observations

00:30:04.412 --> 00:30:06.752
about what is happening in him.”.

00:30:06.752 --> 00:30:12.285
In 1985, Honda was not only taking a bite out of US auto sales, it was selling

00:30:12.285 --> 00:30:17.458
vehicles in other categories that exceeded all domestic production. Honda

00:30:17.458 --> 00:30:22.919
sold more motorcycles, three wheeled all-terrain vehicles and mopeds than any

00:30:22.919 --> 00:30:28.595
other company in the 1980s. While their Spree was an inexpensive, stripped down,

00:30:28.595 --> 00:30:34.128
reliable knock off of a Vespa, the latter barely made a dimple in the American

00:30:34.128 --> 00:30:39.373
market. Honda basically reinvented the fairing clad scooter in the US, and

00:30:39.373 --> 00:30:44.403
turned young riders into long term customers who would go on to buy CBR

00:30:44.403 --> 00:30:50.223
600s, and perhaps a few would buy an NSX. So strong was this company, built by the

00:30:50.223 --> 00:30:55.756
son of a blacksmith in Japan, that it would come to eventually hire one of the

00:30:55.756 --> 00:31:01.504
most respected singer songwriters in the world to subtly star in its advertising.

00:31:01.504 --> 00:31:06.338
I first saw the print ad when I was eleven years old, in a copy of Rolling

00:31:06.338 --> 00:31:11.569
Stone that one of my cousins left at my house. Lou Reed was standing proud, with

00:31:11.569 --> 00:31:16.468
a pair of wire sunglasses on, with his elbows out and his hands in the back

00:31:16.468 --> 00:31:21.368
pocket of his tight jeans. He was standing in front of the NYC skyline on a

00:31:21.368 --> 00:31:26.334
warm evening at dusk, with his legs spread just beside a pair of twin towers

00:31:26.334 --> 00:31:31.234
that were shorter than his shins. His black shoes were rolling slightly out

00:31:31.234 --> 00:31:36.200
like he was stretching from a long ride on the red Honda Elite Deluxe on its

00:31:36.200 --> 00:31:41.100
kickstand to our left. It looked like a Countach in danger of falling in on

00:31:41.100 --> 00:31:46.265
itself. The photograph appeared to have a strong setting sun as a light source,

00:31:46.265 --> 00:31:51.297
although I’m sure it was a tungsten bulb and a gold reflector. It was cropped

00:31:51.297 --> 00:31:55.800
square, with slightly pinched corners that had been left irregular to

00:31:55.800 --> 00:31:59.971
demonstrate the artisanry involved. “Don’t settle for walking.”.

00:31:59.971 --> 00:32:05.879
I had no idea who he was, what it really meant and I barely recognized the city.

00:32:05.879 --> 00:32:11.787
But it stuck. Far more than any other ad or article I saw in any magazine during

00:32:11.787 --> 00:32:17.246
that period, I came to associate that particular brand and product with an

00:32:17.246 --> 00:32:22.705
inarticulable coolness. A few years later, when I figured out who Lou Reed

00:32:22.705 --> 00:32:28.688
was, I remembered the ad immediately. Yo! MTV Raps showed a video of The Original

00:32:28.688 --> 00:32:34.072
Wrapper as a lark, my aunt immediately got out her Velvet Underground and

00:32:34.072 --> 00:32:39.307
Transfomer tapes — and I finally understood the meaning of the tagline.

00:32:39.307 --> 00:32:44.841
The Honda Elite was not walking, it wasn’t the wild side, it was the chance

00:32:44.841 --> 00:32:49.104
to scoot through the wild side, to see it in fast forward.

00:32:49.104 --> 00:32:53.457
Gravity is a symmetric force; one proportionally strong based on the

00:32:53.457 --> 00:32:57.745
product of input masses, and one proportionally weakened by greater

00:32:57.745 --> 00:33:02.812
distances. But distance at a particular time is a matter of perspective, and is

00:33:02.812 --> 00:33:07.619
relatively different on rulers and watches. This doesn’t necessarily affect

00:33:07.619 --> 00:33:12.947
every kind of measurement and transaction in a binary way — multiple points of view

00:33:12.947 --> 00:33:17.949
watching a glass falling from a table will be able to agree that gravity was a

00:33:17.949 --> 00:33:23.211
factor in its shattering when it impacts the floor. But it does affect outcomes of

00:33:23.211 --> 00:33:28.084
interactions when measured in situ. Bertrand Russel’s accessible primer, The

00:33:28.084 --> 00:33:33.086
ABC of Relativity explained that “rigid bodies, which we need for measurement,

00:33:33.086 --> 00:33:38.089
are only rigid for certain observers,” “measurements of distances and times do

00:33:38.089 --> 00:33:42.961
not reveal properties of the things measured, but relations of the things to

00:33:42.961 --> 00:33:47.834
the measurer.” There is a difference between this understanding and the more

00:33:47.834 --> 00:33:52.446
fundamental premise of a force based solely on relative mass and assumed

00:33:52.446 --> 00:33:57.579
distance (between Newton and Einstein) but the primary difference to me, to ‘the

00:33:57.579 --> 00:34:02.321
person that the stone affects,’ is metaphoric. The way we see the world is

00:34:02.321 --> 00:34:04.335
based on our time and position.

00:34:04.335 --> 00:34:09.434
It’s not that we are powerless to affect our moments or nearfinities, it is that

00:34:09.434 --> 00:34:14.211
we are not simultaneous. As a related physics phenomenon might explain, ‘we

00:34:14.211 --> 00:34:18.794
cannot know both the position and momentum of a thing at the same time.’

00:34:18.794 --> 00:34:23.829
And, in a related confusion, we cannot detect something in a true sense without

00:34:23.829 --> 00:34:28.928
colliding with it — and that collision will affect its course. The latter is now

00:34:28.928 --> 00:34:34.156
a somewhat revised idea within its native domain, but outside of physics there are

00:34:34.156 --> 00:34:38.933
plenty of examples of the gravity of scrutiny through the Hawthorne effect.

00:34:38.933 --> 00:34:44.215
If I study the 1982 Cadillac Cimmaron through contemporary material available,

00:34:44.215 --> 00:34:49.635
I will not be able to push all of these weeds back into their Chevrolet Cavalier

00:34:49.635 --> 00:34:54.369
roots, or turn the $13,000 luxurious compact into a significantly more

00:34:54.369 --> 00:34:59.514
reasonable $8,000 base Camaro. But we tacitly assume that we can make such a

00:34:59.514 --> 00:35:04.728
difference in one fundamental area: studying history. If I read and think and

00:35:04.728 --> 00:35:10.353
talk and write about the things that have happened, I will better avoid some of the

00:35:10.353 --> 00:35:15.704
bad things that may happen if I turn my attention away from the subject. But we

00:35:15.704 --> 00:35:21.055
don’t really need this weak inducement — some things we have done are worthy of

00:35:21.055 --> 00:35:26.338
contemplation, but there is barely the bandwidth to consider their names — let

00:35:26.338 --> 00:35:31.757
alone their meanings in a plastós past. Imagining the multiverse of perspectives

00:35:31.757 --> 00:35:36.902
on pasts influencing observable presents into all possible futures is voodoo

00:35:36.902 --> 00:35:42.185
historiographic theory. And unnecessary — as each new mistake will be built on

00:35:42.185 --> 00:35:47.604
every past mistake, and every triumph and whole new ones we are just not making.

00:35:47.604 --> 00:35:52.544
Lesser Cadillac failures, like the Caterra and the Allante, have had auto

00:35:52.544 --> 00:35:57.895
executives stuffing wax into their ears and lashing themselves to masts to keep

00:35:57.895 --> 00:36:02.628
from hearing too many siren calls of budget-niche cars — but have been

00:36:02.628 --> 00:36:06.333
wholesale regrettable only in the case-study retelling.

00:36:06.333 --> 00:36:12.431
“Of all the things that come to us by nature we first acquire the potentiality

00:36:12.431 --> 00:36:17.859
and later exhibit the activity (this is plain in the case of the senses; for it

00:36:17.859 --> 00:36:23.148
was not by often seeing or often hearing that we got these senses, but on the

00:36:23.148 --> 00:36:28.646
contrary we had them before we used them, and did not come to have them by using

00:36:28.646 --> 00:36:34.004
them); but the virtues we get by first exercising them, as also happens in the

00:36:34.004 --> 00:36:39.501
case of the arts as well. For the things we have to learn before we can do them,

00:36:39.501 --> 00:36:44.929
we learn by doing them, e.g. men become builders by building and lyreplayers by

00:36:44.929 --> 00:36:50.288
playing the lyre; so too we become just by doing just acts, temperate by doing

00:36:50.288 --> 00:36:53.280
temperate acts, brave by doing brave acts.”.

00:36:53.280 --> 00:37:02.331
Footnote 12. Nicomachean Ethics, Aristotle, 350BCE, translated by W. D.

00:37:02.331 --> 00:37:06.469
Ross, 1908, beginning of book 2.

00:37:06.469 --> 00:37:11.766
J. Fred Woell published a short book for teachers at the turn of the century, and

00:37:11.766 --> 00:37:16.864
a quote from this passage by Aristotle tops a page called Springboards to Jump

00:37:16.864 --> 00:37:22.227
Start Creativity. “For the things we have to learn before we can do them, we learn

00:37:22.227 --> 00:37:27.524
by doing them.” This is an oft repeated loop; how can we learn to do something we

00:37:27.524 --> 00:37:32.688
don’t know how to do by doing it? If I saw this quote floating on some Internet

00:37:32.688 --> 00:37:37.455
meme I would just assume there was someone trying to sell me some new age

00:37:37.455 --> 00:37:42.553
self-help book — it conjures pulling oneself up by their bootstraps; flying by

00:37:42.553 --> 00:37:47.585
squeezing their shoes hard enough. The teacher in me is not super comfortable

00:37:47.585 --> 00:37:52.617
with this, especially as the subjects that I teach tend to have manual skills

00:37:52.617 --> 00:37:57.450
in them. How would my undergraduate jewelry and industrial design students

00:37:57.450 --> 00:38:02.217
feel about being asked to teach themselves how to do the things they need

00:38:02.217 --> 00:38:07.316
to do? Learn to operate the drill press by operating the drill press, learn to

00:38:07.316 --> 00:38:12.281
perform a 2 rail sweep by sweeping 2 rails. This is nearly the definition of

00:38:12.281 --> 00:38:17.710
creativity and invention, doing something that no one (yet) knows how to do. And we

00:38:17.710 --> 00:38:22.478
predict precisely this as students mature, that the burden of learning is

00:38:22.478 --> 00:38:27.774
increasingly on the learner — they become their own students; teaching themselves

00:38:27.774 --> 00:38:32.475
what to learn and learning by teaching it. But where we tend to model by

00:38:32.475 --> 00:38:37.441
demonstrating techniques, we might sometimes fail to let them fail to figure

00:38:37.441 --> 00:38:42.340
out how to figure it out. Regardless of how my students felt, would they be

00:38:42.340 --> 00:38:46.313
better served by more often depriving them of my instruction?

00:38:46.313 --> 00:38:51.054
I’ve read and participated in many meetings where the subject of how we

00:38:51.054 --> 00:38:56.065
learn figures prominently — and each notion seems to be at war with itself.

00:38:56.065 --> 00:39:01.348
The most problematic of which is the idea of ‘brain-based learning.’ What other

00:39:01.348 --> 00:39:06.089
kind can there be? When you’ve all read the same books (or excerpts, or

00:39:06.089 --> 00:39:11.304
summaries,) you tend to start talking to one another in code. And that code is

00:39:11.304 --> 00:39:16.451
sometimes confused for truth. In this case the advice is distilled into ideas

00:39:16.451 --> 00:39:21.801
about how brain images of learners doing specific tasks is correlated with other

00:39:21.801 --> 00:39:26.745
tasks, (like taking notes by hand) lighting up areas in the brain that are

00:39:26.745 --> 00:39:32.231
associated with long term memory. Typing notes, the brain lights up differently. I

00:39:32.231 --> 00:39:37.581
believe the science, but not as the lazy and simple advice that pedagogues give.

00:39:37.581 --> 00:39:43.044
Systems sell. Sometimes doing hard work is hard, but hard is relative. There are

00:39:43.044 --> 00:39:48.231
novels that one finds only pleasure in reading, and novels that you have you

00:39:48.231 --> 00:39:53.556
have to fight to make any sense of. A textbook in a new field can be harder to

00:39:53.556 --> 00:39:58.881
read than either. And the writing of a sophomore critique of a peer’s work can

00:39:58.881 --> 00:40:04.206
be more work than reading any of these. Making sense of something someone else

00:40:04.206 --> 00:40:09.531
has written can be ecstatic, difficult and a universe of decimals between zero

00:40:09.531 --> 00:40:14.856
and one. Translating a thing from one media to another effectively can be even

00:40:14.856 --> 00:40:19.974
more difficult. McLuhan’s ‘cold/hot’ matrix can be very useful in assessing

00:40:19.974 --> 00:40:25.229
how challenging a category is to the intellect — but it’s just too binary for

00:40:25.229 --> 00:40:30.554
a world with inflationary formats. The compression into temperatures is useful

00:40:30.554 --> 00:40:36.087
insofar as it is jarring and gets us to think about differential effects based on

00:40:36.087 --> 00:40:41.343
the package rather than the content. But the metaphor lacks some nuance; what

00:40:41.343 --> 00:40:46.806
about various warms and cools? What about the relative effects of temperature on

00:40:46.806 --> 00:40:52.200
various types of individuals or various cultures based on biology or sociology?

00:40:52.200 --> 00:40:57.594
Are there special cases? Water has some unique properties as it is frozen — are

00:40:57.594 --> 00:41:03.265
there any such phenomena with supercooled media? (Perhaps they are special cases of

00:41:03.265 --> 00:41:08.037
‘flipping’ in the Media Tetrad.) This isn’t to say that McLuhan didn’t

00:41:08.037 --> 00:41:13.293
stipulate that his systems were imperfect lenses — but that as a system it is

00:41:13.293 --> 00:41:17.926
understandable, sellable, buyable, useable. It can be understood and

00:41:17.926 --> 00:41:22.767
generalized without actually counting all the atoms of barionic matter.

00:41:22.767 --> 00:41:27.844
The very idea that there are popular books and intellectual books, popular

00:41:27.844 --> 00:41:33.200
music and classical music, relationships based on love and others on sex, is a

00:41:33.200 --> 00:41:38.138
gross oversimplification. Even more rigorous, complicated dissections of

00:41:38.138 --> 00:41:43.076
culture are synthetic. Usefully synthetic, sure; but the product of sums

00:41:43.076 --> 00:41:47.875
and averages all the same. Our ideas about people that are pulled from

00:41:47.875 --> 00:41:51.700
aggregates aren’t the simple math we present them to be.

00:41:51.700 --> 00:41:57.159
Pynchon wrote that “words are only an eye twitch away from what they stand for,”

00:41:57.159 --> 00:42:02.618
but imagine a world where every object, movement and emotion was frozen in those

00:42:02.618 --> 00:42:07.663
few letters. I have heard a number of visual artists hesitate to say a lot

00:42:07.663 --> 00:42:13.053
about the work they’ve made, preferring the image of the thing itself to be its

00:42:13.053 --> 00:42:17.752
own words. Pynchon was working in a fortunate format in that way, his

00:42:17.752 --> 00:42:23.280
reclusivity doesn’t leave us with a lot of mysteries. While his work is dense and

00:42:23.280 --> 00:42:28.463
surreal and layered, it is long enough not to leave us in need of still more

00:42:28.463 --> 00:42:33.714
words. The viewer of something visual would like some phrases to extend their

00:42:33.714 --> 00:42:38.966
experience of a work into the verbal, perhaps especially because even a large

00:42:38.966 --> 00:42:44.149
work of sculpture or film is tiny, in comparison to a novel. Words, like the

00:42:44.149 --> 00:42:49.332
things they stand for, can be extruded into longer phrases, phrases parceled

00:42:49.332 --> 00:42:54.929
into paragraphs, paragraphs stacked into pages, pages into chapters, chapters into

00:42:54.929 --> 00:42:59.974
divisions, divisions into stories, which may be books. But, with the right

00:42:59.974 --> 00:43:05.433
application of skill and effort, those can be subdivided back into readers, into

00:43:05.433 --> 00:43:10.961
criticisms, into quotes and can fracture into new forms. Each is analogous to the

00:43:10.961 --> 00:43:12.412
thing, but different.

00:43:12.412 --> 00:43:18.412
A simple idea is often stretched into a more complicated one, a phrase turns into

00:43:18.412 --> 00:43:24.036
a novel, a sketch into a skyscraper, a hum into a symphony. The long tend to

00:43:24.036 --> 00:43:29.661
cluster together, they’re hilarious and/or prophetic, frigid and/or swelling

00:43:29.661 --> 00:43:35.436
for touch, consuming and/or repelling — adjectives and verbs compressed around

00:43:35.436 --> 00:43:41.585
little nouns. Adjacent to these hulks are dense complexities, bound into nutshells,

00:43:41.585 --> 00:43:46.685
pregnant singularities separated by great distances from one another.

00:43:46.685 --> 00:43:51.956
Our apertures are something like judgment. Not necessarily the conscious,

00:43:51.956 --> 00:43:57.447
immediate, free-will variety — but on a number of scales that we control and

00:43:57.447 --> 00:44:03.010
which control us. We decide to read or see or do exhibit A, and it provides a

00:44:03.010 --> 00:44:08.647
filter for everything we see (in and out of its own category) for a lifetime —

00:44:08.647 --> 00:44:14.284
with varying intensity, depending on a plastic loop of circumstances. This can

00:44:14.284 --> 00:44:19.336
be a little crippling at times — to consume or not to consume seems to

00:44:19.336 --> 00:44:24.899
control not just the immediate needs and wants that the thing controls on the

00:44:24.899 --> 00:44:30.683
surface, but is an unmarked Choose Your Own Adventure with lasting consequences.

00:44:30.683 --> 00:44:36.393
At least some choices have a branching structure — even if one jumps to another

00:44:36.393 --> 00:44:41.737
tree or root or river, our decision making impulses (and probably systems)

00:44:41.737 --> 00:44:47.228
are based in large part on the elections (or maybe ethical foundations) made

00:44:47.228 --> 00:44:48.033
previously.

00:44:48.033 --> 00:44:52.896
A young woman is staying at her relative’s mansion and is haunted by two

00:44:52.896 --> 00:44:57.965
ghosts who ply her with conflicting advice on the banal. Each is armed with

00:44:57.965 --> 00:45:03.240
sources, citing contradictory NPR and various magazine headlines about whether

00:45:03.240 --> 00:45:08.377
or not people were meant to sit, when exercising is most effective, with the

00:45:08.377 --> 00:45:13.651
woman eventually finding that the couple has ‘died of confusion.’ Their haunts

00:45:13.651 --> 00:45:18.926
eventually drive her out the window, after which she joins the ghosts in their

00:45:18.926 --> 00:45:24.200
pursuit to give well-meaning advice to the home’s inhabitants. This episode of

00:45:24.200 --> 00:45:29.612
Portlandia, skewers habits that most of us have through sarcasm. Underlying this

00:45:29.612 --> 00:45:35.023
tendency to talk about what we’ve heard in the news are some default settings of

00:45:35.023 --> 00:45:40.092
our platforms; we have faith in our sources, we have limited time to assess

00:45:40.092 --> 00:45:45.161
every angle at every depth of every subject, we want to show our peers that

00:45:45.161 --> 00:45:50.641
we are paying attention, we want to help, we have fallible memories, the research

00:45:50.641 --> 00:45:55.915
is often poorly understood by writers who popularize it, the research is often

00:45:55.915 --> 00:46:00.984
suggestive or preliminary, we tend to grab onto and use things that confirm

00:46:00.984 --> 00:46:06.258
existing beliefs from similar sources… Programs like WNYC’s On the Media would

00:46:06.258 --> 00:46:11.601
love for us to shop for our information in far more suspicious and broad ways —

00:46:11.601 --> 00:46:17.013
not jumping onto the latest information from the most accessible of sources. And

00:46:17.013 --> 00:46:22.287
they provide weekly evidence of how many sources (including NPR itself and the

00:46:22.287 --> 00:46:27.561
affiliates which carry their program across the USA) regularly fail to live up

00:46:27.561 --> 00:46:33.110
to the ethical standards that journalists generally learn in school or on the job.

00:46:33.110 --> 00:46:37.836
It’s as insidious as every other necessary laziness we are guilty of —

00:46:37.836 --> 00:46:43.316
including the opposing tendency to assume that everything we hear is untrue, that

00:46:43.316 --> 00:46:44.412
it is all a lie.

00:46:44.412 --> 00:46:49.221
People in museums sometimes love to get right up on top of a painting, even

00:46:49.221 --> 00:46:53.509
breaking invisible barriers and triggering alarms, to see the brush

00:46:53.509 --> 00:46:58.773
strokes. I like to look at the surface of bronze sculptures — noting that if I get

00:46:58.773 --> 00:47:03.841
close enough to a Brancusi I can see the porosity in the casting that was never

00:47:03.841 --> 00:47:08.195
quite chased and polished out. Others stand well back, taking in the

00:47:08.195 --> 00:47:13.328
composition, or comparing works in close proximity to one another (probably with

00:47:13.328 --> 00:47:18.397
curators grinning while watching them on closed circuit cameras.) Some read the

00:47:18.397 --> 00:47:23.530
mildly didactic labels, some search on their iPhones, some listen to tour guides

00:47:23.530 --> 00:47:28.598
and others discuss what they’re seeing with one another (sometimes asking up or

00:47:28.598 --> 00:47:33.277
talking down to more or less educated companions.) But even if we were to

00:47:33.277 --> 00:47:37.956
capture the people who have similar habits of consumption we would likely

00:47:37.956 --> 00:47:42.699
find that each would learn something different. The facts would likely not

00:47:42.699 --> 00:47:47.703
conflict, but the interpretations (as acquired from critics and friends and as

00:47:47.703 --> 00:47:52.446
formed in the critical faculties of the consumer’s mind) could be Die Hard

00:47:52.446 --> 00:47:53.096
different.

00:47:53.096 --> 00:47:58.626
“Recent studies have shown that ancient studies have been confirmed that hot tubs

00:47:58.626 --> 00:48:01.081
can significantly increase your income.”.

00:48:01.081 --> 00:48:06.315
Our memes have contracted to comedic tropes, the entire observation conflated

00:48:06.315 --> 00:48:11.480
with the products of purpose-built viral marketing campaigns. Dawkins likely

00:48:11.480 --> 00:48:15.681
wouldn’t recognize things on quickmeme.com as containing solid

00:48:15.681 --> 00:48:21.121
examples of the phenomenon he identified. A few small bits of this code may move

00:48:21.121 --> 00:48:26.493
around, occasionally being expressed in social patterns — but for the most part

00:48:26.493 --> 00:48:31.865
the only thing about what we call memes that is memetic is the concept of memes

00:48:31.865 --> 00:48:37.168
itself — our recognition of and search for emergent properties in our culture.

00:48:37.168 --> 00:48:42.264
It’s popsci as brand, Sudoku for people looking to sharpen their ability to

00:48:42.264 --> 00:48:46.740
recognize new shapes in the psychomagnetic foam zeitgeist. Or just

00:48:46.740 --> 00:48:52.181
people who like unexpected quotes mapped onto photos of cats. While most of this

00:48:52.181 --> 00:48:56.933
field is beyond me, it has the malleable softness that attracted me to

00:48:56.933 --> 00:49:02.373
psychoanalysis in college. We copy, at first blindly, and then with a success or

00:49:02.373 --> 00:49:07.745
conformist bias, we mutate, some survive, are copied with or without biases and

00:49:07.745 --> 00:49:11.808
this part of our evolution can eventually alter our biology.

00:49:11.808 --> 00:49:16.927
Boyd and Richerson put it that we are “clever social animals in which learning

00:49:16.927 --> 00:49:21.979
from each other played a negligible role until the evolution of our brain was

00:49:21.979 --> 00:49:27.097
complete, at which point the souped-up chimpanzee was able to take up culture.

00:49:27.097 --> 00:49:31.817
First we got human nature by genetic evolution, then culture arose as an

00:49:31.817 --> 00:49:36.803
evolutionary byproduct.” We all have approximately the same capacity, but an

00:49:36.803 --> 00:49:42.054
interplay of choice and circumstance help us to produce significant variation in

00:49:42.054 --> 00:49:47.372
what and how we are. Consider educational attainment. I tend to see my formal and

00:49:47.372 --> 00:49:52.225
informal education as a functions of my curiosity and determination; I was

00:49:52.225 --> 00:49:57.543
terrible at hitting a ball with a bat, I liked words and drawing, so I eventually

00:49:57.543 --> 00:50:02.795
overcame some of my issues with authority and self-control to achieve a moderate

00:50:02.795 --> 00:50:07.780
habit of learning which progressed through a few degrees, including one at a

00:50:07.780 --> 00:50:12.766
public Ivy. There are anecdotes about being the first in my family to attend

00:50:12.766 --> 00:50:17.685
and graduate from college, about my parents’ income, about grants and loans

00:50:17.685 --> 00:50:23.136
and the accessibility of the first school I went to, the quality of their teaching,

00:50:23.136 --> 00:50:28.188
and about my individual choices. But there are also interrelated factors that

00:50:28.188 --> 00:50:32.975
helped with my success and failure; including the country and state I was

00:50:32.975 --> 00:50:38.160
born into, the county and town I went to high school in, my gender and my race.

00:50:38.160 --> 00:50:42.879
The rough category I fit into is often called privileged. But a powerful

00:50:42.879 --> 00:50:48.264
privilege that wouldn’t come immediately to mind is the size of my nuclear family.

00:50:48.264 --> 00:50:54.166
“The effect of sibship on intelligence, especially in terms of verbal ability, is

00:50:54.166 --> 00:50:58.490
fairly large, even when father’s education (as a partial control for

00:50:58.490 --> 00:51:03.136
innate aspects of intelligence) was controlled for statistically. Youth’s

00:51:03.136 --> 00:51:08.040
educational aspirations are directly and indirectly affected by sibship size,

00:51:08.040 --> 00:51:13.203
which in turn negatively affects a wide variety of extracurricular pursuits, such

00:51:13.203 --> 00:51:17.784
as amount of time spent in cultural activities and reading… Considerable

00:51:17.784 --> 00:51:22.753
evidence suggests that people who get advanced education tend to be from small

00:51:22.753 --> 00:51:23.463
families.”.

00:51:23.463 --> 00:51:32.149
Footnote 13. Rob Boyd and Peter J. Richerson, Not by Genes Alone, 2006, page

00:51:32.149 --> 00:51:32.612
178.

00:51:32.612 --> 00:51:36.318
A generation or two before I was born, combinations of my

00:51:36.318 --> 00:51:42.341
Irish-Italian-Catholic-Working-Class-Midwest-American family may well have given me a larger

00:51:42.341 --> 00:51:47.173
sibgroup than 2. (In fact, my mother was one of six [four of whom survived

00:51:47.173 --> 00:51:51.740
childhood,] and my father one of three.) Not that this would have been

00:51:51.740 --> 00:51:57.167
prescriptive for me in terms of education — I certainly know a few people with MUCH

00:51:57.167 --> 00:52:02.462
larger families where a broad number of individuals within the sibling group have

00:52:02.462 --> 00:52:07.492
attained higher levels of education and/or more success than I have. But as a

00:52:07.492 --> 00:52:12.720
member of a sibclass, my case seems to have been helped (in at least the measure

00:52:12.720 --> 00:52:17.817
of higher ed achievement,) by being one of two. And I have always guessed that

00:52:17.817 --> 00:52:22.979
this was amplified when my brother died as we entered adulthood — the surviving

00:52:22.979 --> 00:52:27.943
attention and resources passed to me. And I believe I was helped by having a

00:52:27.943 --> 00:52:33.039
chemically dependent father — there is nothing like seeing someone you have so

00:52:33.039 --> 00:52:38.400
much in common with so completely destroy their life to motivate you to find other

00:52:38.400 --> 00:52:39.393
ways of living.

00:52:39.393 --> 00:52:44.251
When I was twelve, I wrote a short story about a giant shuttle plucking a star

00:52:44.251 --> 00:52:49.109
from deep space in order to fuse it into our dying sun — I think it was called

00:52:49.109 --> 00:52:53.462
Star Catcher. (And then I might have written Star Catcher II — we were

00:52:53.462 --> 00:52:58.446
incentivized to read and write with free candy masquerading as granola bars.) My

00:52:58.446 --> 00:53:03.052
sense of scale was a little off, and I think the lead hull would have been

00:53:03.052 --> 00:53:07.657
slightly less successful than I’d imagined — but there was something about

00:53:07.657 --> 00:53:12.768
that giant ball of radioactive fire that I needed to make sense of. I was probably

00:53:12.768 --> 00:53:17.689
just beyond the ‘four elements’ theory of matter, certainly not smart enough to

00:53:17.689 --> 00:53:22.294
know that the sun was largely made of hydrogen and helium. Making sense of

00:53:22.294 --> 00:53:26.900
gases lighting up the earth from a hundred million miles away is too crazy

00:53:26.900 --> 00:53:31.505
for me to understand even now — so my twelve year old’s stars were made of

00:53:31.505 --> 00:53:32.010
uranium.

00:53:32.010 --> 00:53:37.481
Uranium is a heavy white metal when refined from its ore; hard enough to

00:53:37.481 --> 00:53:43.106
scratch glass, but soft enough to be formed and forged. We made our yellow

00:53:43.106 --> 00:53:49.270
ceramic glazes and colored our drinking glasses with it until well after we found

00:53:49.270 --> 00:53:55.434
that it exposed a photographic plate in the absence of light. Two of the isotopes

00:53:55.434 --> 00:54:01.136
of this unstable element, 238 and 235, (the potato and the vodka,) share an

00:54:01.136 --> 00:54:07.300
atomic number and are nearly identical. They differ only in minor measurements of

00:54:07.300 --> 00:54:13.618
mass and major potentialities of fission. They are painstakingly distilled from one

00:54:13.618 --> 00:54:19.782
another by physical and chemical means — bringing up the less than one percent of

00:54:19.782 --> 00:54:25.022
235, gradually increasing its concentration as the light outruns (and

00:54:25.022 --> 00:54:31.032
outspins) the heavy. Bombarding uranium 235 with low energy neutrons causes its

00:54:31.032 --> 00:54:37.042
nucleus to split, and its slow initial fission starts a drunken chain reaction.

00:54:37.042 --> 00:54:42.667
The simple is volatilized complex. Uranium is nature — it is the ground we

00:54:42.667 --> 00:54:48.754
build our homes on and the mountains we stare at when we look for peace. It’s in

00:54:48.754 --> 00:54:54.379
the 430,000,000 troy ounces of silver we recycled after using it as magnet

00:54:54.379 --> 00:55:00.312
windings to separate the 238 from the 235 during the Manhattan Project — still

00:55:00.312 --> 00:55:06.168
being cast and stamped into our jewelry and plated onto our flatware. It is a

00:55:06.168 --> 00:55:11.870
number: 92 of 118, a few parts per million of the material universe. A shot

00:55:11.870 --> 00:55:17.803
of schnapps in an ocean, with outsized effects on a few carbon based lives and

00:55:17.803 --> 00:55:23.274
their symbolic systems. Uranium is the word for unending energy, for the

00:55:23.274 --> 00:55:29.284
existential crisis of distillation, for the invisible, for shadows of vaporized

00:55:29.284 --> 00:55:34.215
Japanese, for the end, for signs sitting in for the sidereal, for

00:55:34.215 --> 00:55:35.679
transubstantiation.

00:55:35.679 --> 00:55:41.358
It is also my embarrassing yellow cake — a poorly chosen material McGuffin for an

00:55:41.358 --> 00:55:46.753
out-of-shape adolescent exercise. It marks the year 1986, makes Ronald Reagan

00:55:46.753 --> 00:55:52.361
and Mikhail Gorbachev film-negative blue, it is the slowed down light I see when

00:55:52.361 --> 00:55:55.555
looking backward through the medium of memory.

00:55:55.555 --> 00:56:00.063
Our knobs are vestments of faith in material and language, mechanisms

00:56:00.063 --> 00:56:04.704
hoisting fine print to the heavens, telling gods to go fuck themselves.

00:56:04.704 --> 00:56:09.849
We can go or stay home, watch from wherever we are, be there in streams, go

00:56:09.849 --> 00:56:15.272
or leave, but almost always with hard steps. We have switches to turn ourselves

00:56:15.272 --> 00:56:20.557
on and off, we twist valves and dials connected to potentiometers to cool the

00:56:20.557 --> 00:56:25.493
hot, we swap one wave’s length for another’s height, and we link what we

00:56:25.493 --> 00:56:30.013
control together and call it intelligence. We label our instrument

00:56:30.013 --> 00:56:35.366
panels with vainglorious and nihilistic glyphs, and every time we touch one we

00:56:35.366 --> 00:56:40.442
cede control to the engineers behind them. We make decisions as if systems

00:56:40.442 --> 00:56:41.485
were the world.

00:56:41.485 --> 00:56:46.674
Our instruments, grids and conventions might be there to help us, but they are

00:56:46.674 --> 00:56:51.257
as much client as slave. Drafting subdivides planes in an implied and

00:56:51.257 --> 00:56:56.379
compressed universe, cropped finite to keep the terror from cascading off the

00:56:56.379 --> 00:57:01.770
page and blanketing the earth. From the top we plan — ordering things that can be

00:57:01.770 --> 00:57:06.757
dropped into our dreamscapes without obscuring others. It’s not a realistic

00:57:06.757 --> 00:57:11.677
two-dimensional snapshot, but a linear cross section. We transfer these to

00:57:11.677 --> 00:57:16.933
elevations — describing structure and facade with surreally square perspective.

00:57:16.933 --> 00:57:21.718
We match up features from one view to the next, switching our minds from

00:57:21.718 --> 00:57:27.109
orthographic tops to fronts — imagining volumes where only areas exist. The trick

00:57:27.109 --> 00:57:32.433
is to constrain the variables, to balance precision with the weight of having to

00:57:32.433 --> 00:57:37.757
predict and communicate every atom. We have to keep the topologies simple enough

00:57:37.757 --> 00:57:40.790
for supercomputers. The pen divides the plane,

00:57:40.790 --> 00:57:43.066
but only so long as we believe in it.

00:57:43.066 --> 00:57:48.708
There are eight hundred and eighty four days until I would have would have been

00:57:48.708 --> 00:57:54.206
here, shrinking before the world for thirty years. It’s been time I’ve ticked

00:57:54.206 --> 00:58:00.065
off with a few rites, trials, and scars; a nice round number rolling right off the

00:58:00.065 --> 00:58:05.490
side of a fucking cliff. And at the bottom? Something worse than death, more

00:58:05.490 --> 00:58:11.060
terrifying than inaction: respectability. I’ve been warned of course; in notes

00:58:11.060 --> 00:58:16.413
written to my future self. In scrawl amazingly more legible than my current

00:58:16.413 --> 00:58:22.056
script, I captured what should have been the organizing principle of my life in

00:58:22.056 --> 00:58:27.336
two words: “never forget.” But late adolescence proved less trying for me,

00:58:27.336 --> 00:58:32.110
something approaching happy and independent. Rocks beneath but high

00:58:32.110 --> 00:58:37.608
tides, space between bars to grow. As I’ve aged I’ve noticed that emergencies

00:58:37.608 --> 00:58:43.540
seem farther apart from one another — and never seem quite as intense as they did I

00:58:43.540 --> 00:58:48.820
was younger. Which has tricked me for some time into thinking that life is

00:58:48.820 --> 00:58:54.462
better. But how can it be? Everyone I want dead is still alive, and most of the

00:58:54.462 --> 00:59:00.032
people who meant anything to me are gone. Maybe I’m slowly resigning myself to

00:59:00.032 --> 00:59:05.313
adulthood, to pragmatism, to fear, to a postured religion of artmaking, to

00:59:05.313 --> 00:59:10.594
marriage, to discomfort, to pain, to silence, injustice, to threat without

00:59:10.594 --> 00:59:12.836
strength, to the theft of play.

00:59:12.836 --> 00:59:19.181
Maybe I just need new material, something that fits better with the aging me; soft

00:59:19.181 --> 00:59:25.213
and weak. A hinge that looks like it works but functions only as a noisemaker.

00:59:25.213 --> 00:59:31.167
Something that says ‘I am powerless to change this world, at the mercy of the

00:59:31.167 --> 00:59:34.849
slightest breeze, susceptible to every poison.’.

00:59:34.849 --> 00:59:40.246
I need to be making textured armbands, devoid of any identifiable symbols

00:59:40.246 --> 00:59:45.943
whatsoever; reinventing the universal icon of the adult — noisy neutral. I am

00:59:45.943 --> 00:59:51.940
drawn with a crayon. Weed-like. Leaning toward those images and those words which

00:59:51.940 --> 00:59:57.637
humble, empower and castrate me. Toward whatever light source seems to be the

00:59:57.637 --> 01:00:03.558
truest color, the most honest. Right now I’m a thin blue line surrounded by sky.

01:00:03.558 --> 01:00:09.405
Unexceptional, inarticulate, smudged. Animated only to convince what few people

01:00:09.405 --> 01:00:15.252
listen that I am unfit for living — that I’ve ‘lov’d not wisely but too well.’.

01:00:15.252 --> 01:00:18.456
Cold, hungry, alone,

01:00:18.456 --> 01:00:21.103
whispering and screaming would-be words,

01:00:21.103 --> 01:00:23.936
fighting, dancing, drawing, fucking,

01:00:23.936 --> 01:00:26.815
killing well, before murders invention,

01:00:26.815 --> 01:00:28.580
we were darkness.

01:00:28.580 --> 01:00:30.577
This is not our first turn,

01:00:30.577 --> 01:00:32.713
we’ve had our pile stacked before,

01:00:32.713 --> 01:00:35.592
alternating elements drowning in saltwater;

01:00:35.592 --> 01:00:38.471
foaming ourselves dry with unharnessed energy,

01:00:38.471 --> 01:00:41.350
we are toxic, powdery bedrock.

01:00:41.350 --> 01:00:44.647
From still to trembling, lost to swaggering,

01:00:44.647 --> 01:00:47.851
our empty lungs fill brass cones with color,

01:00:47.851 --> 01:00:51.334
our blurred minds send tired bodies diving,

01:00:51.334 --> 01:00:55.003
dropping Terpsichore’s lyre as her arms are cleaved off,

01:00:55.003 --> 01:00:58.486
and the juddering world has never sounded better.

01:00:58.486 --> 01:01:01.737
Our caves are lit, but we are still silhouettes,

01:01:01.737 --> 01:01:05.359
swaying in front of our stories with hands on collars,

01:01:05.359 --> 01:01:07.820
trailers for an everlasting film,

01:01:07.820 --> 01:01:10.653
cut from a cache of unlabeled movements,

01:01:10.653 --> 01:01:14.136
knit vainglory in ribbons with slow fades.

01:01:14.136 --> 01:01:19.112
The misused is still used. With enough force, every mechanism is capable of

01:01:19.112 --> 01:01:24.424
retroflexion. The leg turns from lever to fringe, a screen from fire to freezer,

01:01:24.424 --> 01:01:29.064
the tool come weapon. It’s not necessarily failure, it’s an engineered

01:01:29.064 --> 01:01:33.772
refrain. Nothing can be built to withstand everything. The universe has

01:01:33.772 --> 01:01:38.748
truck with machines used in ways the creator hadn’t intended, madstone lust

01:01:38.748 --> 01:01:44.060
for entropy. The typewriter has turned from vessel of annunciation to well oiled

01:01:44.060 --> 01:01:49.440
brick in a dry vitrine, but oxygen will find the iron on a long enough timescale.

01:01:49.440 --> 01:01:54.214
Our energies aren’t so much changing form as they are changing function.
